# B2. The filter that keeps a path which dropped records from being called
# fastest.
#
# The one rule `ring_bench`'s recommendation depends on. Remove it and the
# harness recommends whichever candidate discarded the workload quickest, while
# the number it prints still looks entirely reasonable. The failure is a wrong
# recommendation rather than a wrong-looking one, which is why it needs a probe
# rather than a reading.
#
# The round predicted two failures and got six: five behavioural plus the
# `Comparison::fastest` doctest. Four of the five are tests written for other
# purposes that happen to pin `fastest()` on a lossy workload along the way, so
# the rule is defended in depth mostly by accident. The real detector is an
# assertion shape, not a test name. F1 taught the same lesson independently.
#
# The count below is nextest's own, so it excludes the doctest that also failed.

id: B2
crate: ring_bench
file: src/lib.rs
round: 2026-08-28
round-failures: 5
detector: a_path_that_dropped_records_is_not_eligible_to_be_fastest

--- from
      .filter(|outcome| outcome.is_lossless())
--- to
      // .filter(|outcome| outcome.is_lossless())   // PROBE ONLY — G12
--- end
